Venous Leg Ulcer
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Medial malleolus location, Surrounding lipodermatosclerosis/haemosiderin, Relief with leg elevation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-047
Reason for Visit: Rapid increase in malodorous discharge and severe, throbbing pain in a chronic leg wound causing distress and inability to sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a chronic lower limb wound that had acutely developed severe pain, copious malodorous exudate, and surrounding heat. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute secondary bacterial cellulitis overlying chronic venous disease, and to provide immediate targeted antibiotic therapy and wound management to prevent systemic sepsis before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My leg ulcer is weeping right through the bandages, it smells terrible, and the throbbing pain gets worse when I stand up."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-month history of a shallow, irregularly shaped skin ulceration located predominantly over the medial malleolus of the right leg. The wound has historically been mildly painful, with dull aching pain reliably relieved by leg elevation. However, over the past 48 hours, the patient has noted a significant increase in thick, sloughy exudate, accompanied by a new, sharp throbbing pain. The surrounding skin has a chronic history of lipodermatosclerosis and profound haemosiderin staining leading to brown hyperpigmentation. The patient denies systemic fever or chills, but the sudden change in wound characteristics and pain levels prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Independent, but mobility is currently reduced due to leg heaviness and pain upon standing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of deep tissue infection and progressive loss of mobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, sitting with the right leg elevated on a footstool.
Physical Exam: The right lower leg demonstrates severe, chronic venous insufficiency changes, including marked haemosiderin staining (brown hyperpigmentation) and "inverted champagne bottle" induration (lipodermatosclerosis). Mild pedal pitting oedema is present. Situated over the medial malleolus is a 4x5 cm shallow, irregularly shaped ulcer. The base contains prominent granulation tissue mixed with thick, malodorous, yellow sloughy exudate. The skin immediately surrounding the ulcer exhibits a new 3 cm rim of acute, tender, blanching erythema and increased localized heat. Palpable and normal distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are present.
Environmental Observations: Soaked, inadequate wound dressings visible in the rubbish bin.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient uses compression stockings but has been non-compliant for a week due to hot weather.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Venous Leg Ulcer with acute localized secondary infection (Cellulitis) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Arterial Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of strong, palpable pedal pulses, the medial malleolus location, the relief of pain with elevation (arterial ulcers worsen with elevation), and the classic surrounding venous skin changes.

Diabetic Neuropathic Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The significant, throbbing pain (neuropathic ulcers are painless), the specific malleolar location rather than pressure points on the plantar surface, and the prominent venous staining.

Pyoderma Gangrenosum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a rapidly expanding, deeply undermined violaceous border, and the clear presence of underlying chronic venous insufficiency driving the pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acutely infected chronic venous leg ulcer, characterized by a sudden increase in exudate, malodour, and surrounding cellulitic erythema. Triage correctly identified the sudden, severe pain and heavily weeping wound as requiring urgent medical evaluation. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was vital to physically rule out deep tissue involvement or osteomyelitis, achieve source control by cleansing the highly exuding wound, and initiate targeted oral antibiotics. Intervening now successfully aborted a rapidly progressing superficial cellulitis, alleviating the patient's severe pain and averting an impending emergency hospital admission for intravenous therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Thoroughly cleansed the ulcer base with sterile saline, mechanically removing loose slough. Applied a highly absorbent alginate dressing to manage the heavy exudate, secured with a firm, non-adherent secondary bandage. Prescribed oral Flucloxacillin 500mg QID for 7 days to treat the surrounding cellulitis.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your antibiotics exactly four times a day on an empty stomach, you will guarantee a steady stream of medicine reaches your leg to kill the spreading bacteria. By strictly keeping your leg elevated above your heart whenever you are sitting, you will use gravity to drain the painful swelling, which immediately reduces the throbbing pressure and is absolutely necessary for the ulcer to heal.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral sent to the community wound care clinic for ongoing dressing management and re-initiation of compression therapy once the acute infection subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): Leg elevation pillow recommended.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to assess the cellulitis response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red, hot skin rapidly spreads up your calf towards your knee, if the leg becomes completely swollen and severely painful, or if you develop a high fever and uncontrollable shivering, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Venous ulcers are notoriously slow to heal and require long-term management of your underlying vein problems to prevent recurrence.
Clinical Course: The sharp throbbing pain and the bad smell should improve within 48-72 hours on antibiotics. The ulcer itself will take many weeks of dedicated dressing changes and compression to close.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the red skin spreading away from the ulcer edge. Seek emergency care if this redness rapidly expands or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your leg veins are struggling to pump blood back to your heart, causing fluid to pool and eventually break open the skin. A bacterial infection has now entered that open wound. The antibiotics will kill the infection, but elevating your leg is the only way to reduce the fluid pressure allowing the wound to breathe and heal.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing completely dry. Do not attempt to reapply your tight compression stockings until the acute infection and severe pain have settled in a few days.

